      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please do not `#undef CONFIG_HWTIMER_64BIT`, `#define` internal symbols (`get_time`, `timer_us_to_ticks`, `clksrc_high`), and `#include \"../../../../common/timer.c\"` inside a Zephyr test:\n\n1. **Why this is problematic**:\n   - Textually `#include`-ing a `.c` file with preprocessor macro rewrites bypasses Kconfig/CMake and compiles a synthetic translation unit that cannot exist in any real build.\n   - More importantly, the `!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HWTIMER_64BIT)` branch in `common/timer.c` is **unreachable dead code on `main`**:\n     - `common/timer.c` is only compiled when `CONFIG_PLATFORM_EC_TIMER\u003dy` (`zephyr/CMakeLists.txt:583`).\n     - `zephyr/shim/include/config_chip.h` unconditionally sets `#define CONFIG_HWTIMER_64BIT` whenever `CONFIG_PLATFORM_EC_TIMER` is enabled (because Zephyr timers are 64-bit).\n     - `force_time()`, `timer_init()`, and `process_timers()` have already been deleted from `common/timer.c` on `main`, so `clksrc_high` is **never written anywhere in the codebase** on `main` (it is permanently `0`).\n\n2. **Recommended fix**:\n   - The real 32-bit hardware timer overflow bug is on `ec-legacy` (covered by `crrev.com/c/8605930`).\n   - On `main`, this CL should either be abandoned or repurposed to delete the dead `clksrc_high` variable and `!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HWTIMER_64BIT)` branch from `common/timer.c`.",
